/page --pageless

# Pagination Control in Polyester

Polyester gives you control over how your document is paginated when generating PDFs.

---

## Pageless Mode

This document uses `/page --pageless` which creates a **continuous, scrolling PDF** instead of breaking content into fixed A4 pages.

This is useful when:
- You want to prevent tables or figures from being split across pages
- The document is primarily meant for screen viewing
- You want precise control over where page breaks occur

/frame --bg "#f0f9ff" --border "1px solid #0ea5e9" {
  /code polyester {
    /page --pageless

    Your content flows continuously...
  }
}

---

## Explicit Page Breaks

Even in pageless mode, you can insert explicit page breaks using `/pagebreak`:

/frame --bg "#f0fdf4" --border "1px solid #22c55e" {
  /code polyester {
    # Section 1
    Content for section one...

    /pagebreak

    # Section 2
    This starts on a new page.
  }
}

---

## Example: Keeping Tables Together

One common use case is ensuring tables don't get split across pages:

/table --header --striped --bordered {
  Feature | Description
  `/page --pageless` | Creates a continuous PDF without automatic page breaks
  `/pagebreak` | Forces a page break at that location
  Default behavior | A4 pages with automatic breaks
}

Without `--pageless`, a long table might be split awkwardly between pages. With pageless mode, the table stays intact and you control exactly where breaks occur.

/pagebreak

# Page 2: After Explicit Break

This content appears on a new page because we used `/pagebreak` above.

## Usage Examples

### Standard Paginated Document (Default)

/frame {
  /code polyester {
    /page A4 --margin 2cm

    Content is automatically split into A4 pages.
  }
}

### Continuous Document

/frame {
  /code polyester {
    /page --pageless

    Content flows as one long page.
    Use /pagebreak for manual control.
  }
}

### Landscape Paginated

/frame {
  /code polyester {
    /page letter --landscape --margin 1in

    Wide content on landscape letter pages.
  }
}

---

## When to Use Each Mode

/columns 2 {
  /card --title "Use Pageless When:" {
    /list {
      Tables shouldn't split
      Screen-first viewing
      You want manual break control
      Creating scrollable reports
    }
  }

  /card --title "Use Default When:" {
    /list {
      Printing on paper
      Standard document flow is fine
      Following page size conventions
      Headers/footers matter
    }
  }
}
